  "live_records": {
    "q-106488": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106488",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273766/assets/f98551299f73f55e9c1a07ef513723f068f66576a514d1895c07f317c10ae262.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=-2$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=-2$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
