    "q-106490": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106490",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273766/assets/d651c43faf3d85411e3aefa905278ff64e45b178f5625a0b92c7c89c3b5eb66f.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=-3$ from the left. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, when we approach $x=-3$ from the right, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-106490-a",
